    "created_at": "2025-10-06T10:25:34Z",
    "updated_at": "2025-10-06T10:25:34Z",
    "body": "Metadata-based orientation or crop isn't really metadata but about storing aspects that are important for how the image looks in part of the file that isn't technically pixel data.\n\nIn that sense, orientation probably should never be removed.\n\nIn theory, it would be good for privacy to throw away (before upload) pixels that are hidden by metadata-based crop. However, this kind of thing (as well as changing the pixels to apply orientation and then resetting orientation) is problematic with things like gain maps: the gain map needs to match the new pixel data dimensions and orientation. A program that flattens orientation or crop needs to know about all the vendor-specific gain map formats or risk corrupting the applicability of the gain map in the process. If a browser wants to make sure that the user doesn't publish secrets that the user thinks they've cropped out, it's probably safer for the browser to overwrite the supposedly-cropped out image compression tiles with black or white than to change the pixel dimensions of the pixel data.\n\nBut more generally, does it really make sense to have this as a browser feature whose parameters the Web site directs?\n\nIt seems to me that redacting metadata as a browser function that the user chooses to use from the file picker makes sense. Also, it makes sense for a site to want to redact metadata. A site could use its own code for this and know what it does.\n\nBut if the site asks the browser to do things on this point, the user can't trust what happens and the site can't trust what  happens.\n\nOverall, editing EXIF data is a hard problem. Even exiftool that specializes in this can't write all the tag types that it can read.",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
    "reactions": {
      "url": "https://api.github.com/repos/whatwg/html/issues/comments/3370923367/reactions",
