    "created_at": "2025-10-07T05:56:37Z",
    "updated_at": "2025-10-07T05:57:46Z",
    "body": "> The main benefit I see is that when the website indicates they don't need the data, the file picker doesn't have to give the user choice on this matter.\n\nIf a user learns that when uploading images (or videos?), there's an option to remove metadata, how would you communicate to the user that not having that choice means that the site has requested metadata removal as opposed to there being some kind of browser-side glitch that fails to show the option and would upload with metadata included?\n\n> And orientation can be flattened as well.\n\nIt's a non-trivial software development project, and it's not clear that the opportunity cost would be justified for a browser development team.\n\nIn general, it seems bad for the browser to silently recompress the pixels. Some formats don't support lossless reorientation of the pixels or support it only if the dimensions are divisible by a particular number. Even lossless recompression of losslessly compressed pixels can be unwanted in case of PNGs whose compression has been optimized.\n\nRegardless of lossless data rearrangement vs. recompressing, the browser would need to support all possible vendor gain map formats in order to be able to transform the gain map in a corresponding way without data loss. exiftran that specializes in lossless reorientation for JPEG predates gain maps and, therefore, breaks gain maps, which is in practice data loss that depending on use case could be considered even be worse than recompression.",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
    "reactions": {
      "url": "https://api.github.com/repos/whatwg/html/issues/comments/3375319156/reactions",
