    "body": "I'd like to be able to declare that a website *needs* specific EXIF metadata. My website relies on people being able to upload geotagged photos. This works on desktop browsers, but recent changes to Android have broken that flow for mobile web browsers . The `<input type=\"file\">` picker now strips all GPS information from photos.\n\nSee various reports [1](https://issues.chromium.org/issues/40287342) [2](https://issuetracker.google.com/issues/330051544) [3](https://issuetracker.google.com/issues/318808004) [4](https://issuetracker.google.com/issues/428397711).\n\nI appreciate this is probably done for privacy reasons, but it seems odd that there's no way for a website to request the \"raw\" image from the file picker.\n\nSimilarly, EXIF is also redacted when capturing directly from the camera using `<input type=\"file\" capture=\"user\" />`\n\nAs a developer, I'd like to be able to add something like:\n\n```html\n<input type=\"file\" \n   accept=\"image/*\" capture=\"user\"\n   geolocation=\"true\"\n/>\n```\n\nOr\n\n```html\n<input type=\"file\" \n   accept=\"image/*\" capture=\"user\"\n   allow=\"exif\"\n/>\n```\n\nOr\n\n```html\nPermissions-Policy: exif=(self \"https://example.com\")\n```\n\nAs a user, I'd like something like a pop-up saying \"example.com wants to know the location of this photo. Allow? Yes / No / Remember this selection\"\n\nBasically the same as all the other permission prompts.",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
